Project Overview
This class is focused on enhancing understanding of Java generics and collections through a project that implements an Inventory Management System. The project is broken down into various milestones, each focusing on different aspects of generics and collections.

Milestone 1: Implementing Item Class Hierarchy
Task Description
Create Base Class: Item with attributes - id, name, price, and quantity.
Create Subclasses: for specific items like Book, Clothing, and Electronics.
Comparison Logic: Implement sorting based on the price using the Comparable interface in the Item class.
Code Examples
Item.java:
public class Item implements Comparable<Item>{
    private String id;
    private String name;
    private double price;
    private int quantity;

    public Item(String id, String name, double price, int quantity) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.quantity = quantity;
    }

    // Getters and setters

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Item o) {
        return Double.compare(this.price, o.price);
    }
}
Book.java, Clothing.java, and Electronics.java:
Extend the Item class.
Add specific attributes like author for Book, size for Clothing, and warranty for Electronics.
Lesson Highlights
Override toString() Method: To provide a cleaner interface.
Use Collections.sort(): For sorting the items by price after implementing the compareTo method【4:0†source】【4:3†source】.
Upper and Lower Bounds in Generics
Understanding Bounds
Upper Bound (extends): Restricts the input parameter to a specific range of classes from a superclass. Useful for creating restrictions, e.g., ensuring the method works with any subclass of Number.
Lower Bound (super): Allows a broader range of inputs while ensuring a minimum level, often used when writing flexible code that requires less specificity for modifications【4:5†source】【4:8†source】.
Practical Applications
Use Cases: Sorting and modifying collections in a type-safe manner.
Example: Implementing a method that can accept a list containing any type of Number but not strings【4:4†source】【4:9†source】【4:11†source】.
Milestone 5: Sorting and Filtering
Task Description
This milestone focuses on sorting and filtering items based on custom criteria using comparator interfaces.

Code Examples
Implement different comparators:
Name Comparator:
public class ItemNameComparator implements Comparator<Item> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Item o1, Item o2) {
        return o1.getName().compareTo(o2.getName());
    }
}
Quantity Comparator:
public class ItemQuantityComparator implements Comparator<Item> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Item o1, Item o2) {
        return Integer.compare(o2.getQuantity(), o1.getQuantity()); // Descending order
    }
}
Lesson Highlights
Comprehensive Sorting: Leveraged custom comparators to meet specific sorting needs.
Flexible Inventory Management: Enabling sorting and filtering based on dynamically applied business logic【4:15†source】【4:17†source】.
Additional Concepts Explored
Wildcard Generics: Using question marks (?) to denote an unknown type in generics, crucial for creating flexible APIs that work with various types【4:6†source】.
Why Use Comparators? Comparators offer a robust way to sort complex objects where multiple sorting algorithms might be needed beyond natural ordering【4:14†source】.
